# Line endings are load-bearing in this repository.
#
# The functional fixture under evals/functional/fixture/repos/ is pushed to
# Azure DevOps and then read back and compared byte-for-byte. A checkout that
# translates LF to CRLF changes those bytes and fails the comparison, so the
# checkout must not translate. core.autocrlf=true is the Git for Windows
# installer default and arrives at system scope on every Windows clone; these
# rules override it per path rather than asking each clone to reconfigure.

* text=auto

*.yml  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.ps1  text eol=lf

# docs/diagram/flow.html is GENERATED, committed, and re-rendered by
# tools/diagram/Build-Diagram.ps1 -Check, which compares a fresh render
# against the committed one. The renderer writes LF; `* text=auto` gave the
# checkout CRLF; the two then differ on every line while the line COUNT stays
# identical, which reads as a content change and is not one. Third instance of
# the hazard this file already documents twice - found by verify.ps1 comparing
# from a fresh clone rather than from the working tree, which is the whole
# reason it clones.
*.html text eol=lf

# Git's own dotfiles match no extension rule above and would fall to
# `* text=auto`, meaning CRLF on a Windows checkout.
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf

# The functional seed is copied byte-for-byte into a run directory by
# Reset-Target.ps1, so its bytes must not depend on where this repository was
# checked out. Three of its four files - .gitattributes, .gitignore and LICENSE
# - match none of the extension rules above and fell to `* text=auto`, which
# means CRLF on any Windows clone. Measured with `git check-attr text eol`:
# README.md resolved to eol=lf and the other three to eol unspecified.
evals/functional/seed/** text eol=lf

# The Terraform seed is the same shape of hazard and gets the same rule.
# Reset-TfTarget.ps1 copies it byte-for-byte into a run directory and the
# resulting TREE SHA is pinned in LEDGER.md, so a checkout that translated
# LICENSE to CRLF would produce a different tree and the pin would fail for a
# reason that has nothing to do with the seed. Measured with
# `git ls-files --eol` before adding this: README.md resolved to eol=lf,
# .gitattributes and .gitignore to eol=lf by the dotfile rules above, and
# LICENSE to `text=auto` with no eol - i.e. CRLF on any Windows clone.
evals/tf/seed/** text eol=lf
